## 2. Where enforcement has to live

Here is a route reviewers approve every day:

```javascript
// BROKEN — for analysis only
app.get("/api/submissions/:id", requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
  const submission = await db.submissions.findById(req.params.id);
  res.json(submission);
});
```

`requireLogin` verifies a valid session. That is authentication, complete and correct. What is *not* checked is whether this requester may see *this* submission — so any authenticated user, meaning every student in the district, can iterate `:id` and read everything.

This is **IDOR** (Insecure Direct Object Reference), also called BOLA: a direct reference to an object with no check that the caller may follow it.

### The two tempting bad fixes

**"The UI only shows students their own submissions, so the id is never wrong in practice."** This is the single most dangerous sentence in authorization work. The UI is a suggestion rendered on a machine the attacker controls; the endpoint is the actual interface, and it is directly callable with arbitrary parameters forever.

**"Move the check into middleware."** Better, and still wrong:

```javascript
app.get("/api/submissions/:id", requireLogin, requireRole("teacher"), handler);
```

This answers *what kind of user* and not *whose record*. Ms. Alvarez is a teacher, and she can now read submissions from every class in the district, including ones she does not teach.

### The correct shape

Role checks can sit in middleware because they need only the identity. Ownership checks **cannot**, because middleware runs before the record is loaded and therefore has nothing to compare against. Object-level decisions belong after the fetch:

```javascript
app.get("/api/submissions/:id", requireLogin, async (req, res) => {
  const submission = await db.submissions.findById(req.params.id);
  if (!submission) return res.sendStatus(404);
  if (!can(req.user, "read", submission)) return res.sendStatus(404);   // 404, not 403
  res.json(submission);
});
```

Authorization has two layers with different homes. A codebase with only the first has the most common vulnerability class in the industry.

